    <!-- https://w3id.org/qpm#GADM -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/qpm#GADM">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/qpm#Hierarchy"/>
        <hierarchyName>GADM</hierarchyName>
        <rdfs:comment>Exemplar abstract hierarchy: the Global Administrative Areas dataset, used as a substitute administrative division of space where an authoritative national hierarchy is missing. Its levels (GADM 0 country, 1 first-level division, 2 second-level, and so on) populate as ordinary Units with containedBy between adjacent levels, exactly as an authoritative hierarchy does. Muhajab additionally uses GADM levels to guide which levels of different hierarchies meaningfully interact when deriving cross-hierarchy relations; that level-correspondence is a method that consumes this hierarchy, not a feature of the hierarchy declaration itself.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>GADM</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/qpm#H3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/qpm#H3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/qpm#Hierarchy"/>
        <hierarchyName>H3</hierarchyName>
        <rdfs:comment>Exemplar abstract hierarchy: the H3 discrete global grid. Grid cells at each resolution are Units; a finer cell is containedBy its coarser parent cell, giving a uniform hierarchical division of space independent of any authoritative boundaries. Places relate to H3 cells with the ordinary containedByUnit relation.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>H3</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


